.Dd November 15, 2026
.Dt RGE 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rge
.Nd Realtek 8125/8125B/8126/8127 PCI Express 10/100/1Gb/2.5Gb/5Gb/10Gb Ethernet device
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "rge* at pci?"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver provides support for NICs based on the
Realtek RTL8125, RTL8126, and RTL8127 PCI
Express Ethernet controllers, including the following:
.Pp
.Bl -bullet -offset indent -compact
.It
IOCrest IO-PCE8126-GLAN Adapter (5000baseT)
.It
WisdPi WP-NA5000 Adapter (5000baseT)
.It
Realtek 8125/8125B 2.5GbE Adapter (2500baseT)
.It
Realtek 8126 5GbE Controller (5000baseT)
.It
Rivet Networks Killer E3000 Adapter (2500baseT)
.It
TP-LINK TL-NG421 Adapter (2500baseT)
.El
.Pp
NICs based on the 8125 and 8125B are capable of 10, 100, 1000 and
2500Mbps operation.
NICs based on the 8126 are also capable of 5000Mbps operation, while
NICs based on the 8127 are additionally capable of 10000Mbps operation.
All NICs support off-loading of checksum calculations for IPv4, TCPv4,
and UDPv4, but not for xxxv6.
.Sh WAKE-ON-LAN
The
.Nm
driver can arm the controller to wake the machine on a magic packet when
the system is powered down.
It is controlled by the sysctl node
.Va hw.rgeN.wol ,
where
.Ar N
is the unit number:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Li 0
The default.
Wake-on-LAN is not armed.
When the driver attaches it clears the magic packet, link change and frame
wakeup settings of the controller, so a setting left by firmware, by
another system or by an earlier power-down does not wake the machine.
.It Li 1
At power-down the driver enables the magic packet wakeup in the
controller, stops the interface if it is running, lets the stopped
controller accept frames to the station, broadcast and multicast frames,
enables power management events in the controller and sets the PCI power
management PME enable bit.
.El
.Pp
Any other value is rejected with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
The controller is armed only when the system is powered off, for example by
.Xr shutdown 8
.Fl p
or
.Xr halt 8
.Fl p ;
a plain reboot or halt leaves it unarmed.
It is not armed on suspend.
If the device has no PCI power management capability, or power management
is disabled in the controller, the driver prints one message at power-down
and leaves the controller alone.
.Pp
Waking the machine also requires standby power on the port and support in
the board firmware, which may have its own setting for waking from the
network.
Neither can be checked by the driver.
.Pp
The Wake-on-LAN code has not been verified on real hardware yet.
